  <data name="en_pano25_description"><![CDATA[Welcome to Nike Art Gallery, Abuja. Whether you are a specialist in the arts, an enthusiast of African arts or merely interested in a brief immersion in a new culture, Nike offers the opportunity to see Nigeria with fresh eyes]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano31_description"><![CDATA[On entry into the gallery one is greeted with and aura of rich ancient African art that are clearly visible on every nock and cranny of the environment. Some of the noticeable crafts on display at the Gallery are, two life sized replicas of Shrines found at the Sacred Grove of Osun Oshogbo, artistic relief on the surrounding walls and on the main building, housing the Gallery.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano43_description"><![CDATA[This section of the galley holds a collection of beautiful works of African art. Ranging from, wood sculptures, beautifully designed Calabash, wooden mask, and hand-made Nigerian Fabrics. When in Abuja take out time to go through Nigerians rich cultural heritage by visiting Nike Art gallery.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano45_description"><![CDATA[This is the Canvas section, it contains a large collections of diverse painting, ranging from contemporary to ancient. Here you will be wowed by the creativity of the human mind. The beauty of the collections in this section cannot be put into words, but can only be experienced by just a visit to the galley.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano47_description"><![CDATA[While in the galley, just be ready because your mind will be playing lots of tricks on you as you may see yourself buying art works that are more than your budget.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano49_description"><![CDATA[This is the Canvas section, it contains a large collections of diverse painting, ranging from contemporary to ancient. Here you will be wowed by the creativity of the human mind. The beauty of the collections in this section cannot be put into words, but can only be experienced by just a visit to the galley.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano51_description"><![CDATA[This is the Textile section of the Gallery, here one will come in contact with beautifully hand mad fabrics ready for sale. Among these textiles are: adire, Quilting, Reverse Appliqué, batik and so much more.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano27_description"><![CDATA[On entry into the gallery one is greeted with and aura of rich ancient African art that are clearly visible on every nock and cranny of the environment. Some of the noticeable crafts on display at the Gallery are, two life sized replicas of Shrines found at the Sacred Grove of Osun Oshogbo, artistic relief on the surrounding walls and on the main building, housing the Gallery.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano29_description"><![CDATA[While in the Gallery, one will see series of red earthen-pots that are stacked on one another, towering about 10meters high. Some of these earthen pot towers are used to support the two replicas of Oshun Oshogbo Sacred Shrines.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano33_description"><![CDATA[The green area of the Gallery is also dotted with several Stone Sculptures that are beautifully finished, their smoothness will leave one wondering if these sculpture are actually hand-made.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano35_description"><![CDATA[<div>Nike found that the traditional methods of weaving and dying that had been her original inspiration were fading in Nigeria. She used her international success to launch a cultural revival, building art centers where young Nigerians master traditional arts and crafts. You can now visit the world of the Yoruba, to explore a culture that has flourished in Western Nigeria for well over 1000 years.</div><div><br></div><div>She is also known for her colorful batik and paintings that offers a modernist gloss on traditional themes</div>]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano37_description"><![CDATA[A little further away from the main gallery building, one will come in contact with the indigo-die pit. Indigo Dye is made by burning cocoa pods to ashes, then filtering water through the ashes into a pot in which indigo leaves are placed and left to ferment in the sun for 7 to 10 days before using as a dye.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano39_description"><![CDATA[Here, One will come in contact with a traditional hut made with red earth, with its supporting pillars made of stacks of clay pots and wood carving.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano41_description"><![CDATA[Replica of Traditional Gwar Grain Bans can also be seen at the Gallery.]]></data>


